<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyaswa of Pagan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1251</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Kyaswa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kyaswa was king of Pagan from 1235 to 1251, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/htilominlo">Htilominlo</a>. Remembered as a learned and pious king devoted to the study of Buddhist scripture, he left government largely to his ministers and gained little new territory or revenue. His reign saw the crown struggle with the growing extent of tax-free religious landholding.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyaswa-of-pagan.jpg" alt="Kyaswa of Pagan" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EmeraldRange · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kyaswa_Statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kyaswa was born to Prince Zeya Theinkha, the future King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/htilominlo">Htilominlo</a>, and his wife Eindawthe. An inscription donated by his mother's younger sister gives his birth as Monday, 4 May 1198, at four in the morning, two weeks later than the 20 April 1198 of the Zatadawbon Yazawin chronicle; the other main chronicles give other dates. On his accession in 1235 he took the regnal name Śrī Tribhuvanāditya Pavarapaṇḍita Dhammarāja.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His reign, like his father's, was largely peaceful, but the drain on the royal treasury from large tax-free religious landholdings grew worse, and the king had difficulty completing a temple. Unlike earlier kings he tried to deal with the problem by taking back some land held by forest-dwelling monks, but public feeling against any seizure of monastic land forced him to return it. Frustrated, he handed the administration to his son and deputies and gave his patronage only to orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> sects. The forest-dwellers neither needed that patronage nor feared his authority, and late in the reign they were openly offering meat and liquor to their followers.</p><p>Kyaswa did not use forced labour to build his temples, and his Pyathada Temple at Pagan is much smaller than many built by his predecessors. In 1249 he issued royal edicts, dated 22 April, 1 May and 6 May, to be carved on stone in every village of more than 50 houses. In them he renounced the tortures, beginning with impaling, by which past kings had punished thieves, and declared that he regarded all beings as his children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The main chronicles place his death between 1249 and 1251. Because his son Uzana died in May 1256 after a reign of five years, Kyaswa most probably died in early 1251, as the horoscope section of the Zatadawbon Yazawin reports, and scholars provisionally accept that year. The kingdom founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a> more than two centuries earlier was still at peace, but it was already in decline and unprepared for the internal disorder and outside pressure that followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kyaswa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bagan, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Bagan, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.17,94.86&amp;year=1241" aria-label="Bagan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Kyaswa of Pagan"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5387 5744h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5387 5744h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.17,94.86&amp;year=1241">What was here in 1241? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
